<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herminafrid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 534</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Hermanfrid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herminafrid was the last king of the Thuringians, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalaberga">Amalaberga</a>, niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>. After Theodoric's death the Frankish kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-i">Theuderic I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> defeated him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> in 531 and annexed his kingdom. He was killed soon afterwards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>, falling from the town walls while under Theuderic's protection.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was one of three sons of King Bisinus and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> queen Menia; his sister Raicunda married the Lombard king Wacho. Between 507 and 511 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalaberga">Amalaberga</a>, a granddaughter of Theodemir, and he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> a gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-white horses. When he became king is unclear, but a letter of Theodoric dated to 507 already calls him king of the Thuringii.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first shared the kingdom with his brothers Baderic and Bertachar. In 529 he killed Bertachar in battle, leaving his young niece <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a> an orphan. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalaberga">Amalaberga</a> then urged him against Baderic, once laying only half the table and telling him that a king who owned half a kingdom deserved half a table bare. He made a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-i">Theuderic I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, against Baderic, who was overcome by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and beheaded, but he refused to keep his side of the bargain, and the two kings became enemies.</p><p>Theuderic, his son Theudebert I and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Clotaire I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> then attacked the Thuringii, won a battle near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> and took the royal seat at Scithingi, the modern Burgscheidungen. The Thuringian king escaped, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a> and his nephews were captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theuderic then gave him safe conduct to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a> and many gifts. While the two talked, someone pushed him from the town walls, and Gregory notes that some people suggested Theuderic had a hand in it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a> was forced to marry Clotaire. Amalaberga fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> with her children Amalafrid and Rodelinda; later captured by the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, she was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where Amalafrid became an imperial general and Rodelinda married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Auduin.</p><p>The Thuringian kingdom ended with him: Slavic tribes took the land east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Its fall became the subject of many epic retellings, above all in the Saxon foundation myth that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> wrote in 967, in which Saxons under Duke Hadugato win the battle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> as allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Gregory, the main source, gives the Frankish view; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind">Widukind</a> wrote much later and drew on myth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hermanafrid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 6th century King <b>Herminafrid</b> married Amalaberga, niece of the Ostrogothic king Theodoric the Great, linking Thuringia to the Ostrogothic alliance system.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-thuringians">Kingdom of the Thuringians</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.7,6.65&amp;year=524" aria-label="Zülpich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Herminafrid"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5359 5774h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5359 5774h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.7,6.65&amp;year=524">What was here in 524 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
